\begin_layout Title
Experimental Methods I
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 3, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The three readings provided background information and examples concerning
 economic behavior and experimental methods.
 The common theme among all of the texts was the importance of a solid experimen
tal design.
 Two of the most significant aspects to consider when constructing an experiment
 are: identifying the focus variables of interest and the nuisance variables
 that could affect the outcome of results; selecting the proper treatment
 variables and those you wish to control by holding constant.
 Some of the efficient designs described were: randomization, random block,
 crossover, dual trial, and factorial design.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Chapter 9 of Davis and Holt added the very helpful discussion of the relationshi
p among theoretical, experimental, and natural economic experiments.
 Accordingly, an experimental design should coincide with the particular
 test the researcher is considering, whether it be a theory, component,
 or stress test, or a search for empirical regularities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

No matter the type of experiment, almost all of them employ the use of human
 subjects, which leads to a unique set of problems when collecting and analyzing
 data.
 So, it is necessary for researchers to maintain an acceptable level of
 control over the economic environments, institutions, etc.
 Above all, it is critical that particpants receive salient rewards that
 depend on their actions.
 In addition, changes in subjects' incentives and utility should predominantly
 come from the corresponding reward structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, it is imperative for researchers to hone their data analysis skills,
 so they can make the proper statistical inferences in regards to their
 hypotheses.
 Experimental data should be evaluated using two approaches.
 Qualitative methods, which primarily employ graphs and summary statistics,
 are intended to give an overview of the data.
 On the other, qualitative tools, such as inferential statistics, give answers
 to specific questions.
 Remember to utilize the correct statistical inference test depending on
 the kind of data that is collected.
\end_layout

